<div class="migration-page">
<header><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / <a href="/resources/blog/">Blog</a> / Social media performance</nav><h1>How to Analyze Your Social Media Performance</h1><p class="intro">A busy social feed is not the same as a useful one. Review what people saw, how they responded and whether those interactions helped your business achieve its goals.</p></header>
<article>
<section><h2>Start with one business question</h2><p>Decide what you need to learn before opening a dashboard. Are your posts helping prospective customers understand your work? Are people asking relevant questions? Are visitors reaching your website and making qualified inquiries?</p><p>Choose a reporting period and compare it with a similar period. Record changes in advertising spend, posting frequency, seasonality and service availability. These differences can explain a change in results without proving that one post caused it.</p></section>
<section><h2>1. Read engagement in context</h2><p>Comments, reactions, shares, saves and clicks can show how people respond to content. Review the actual response as well as the count. A complaint and a recommendation both create activity, but they call for different next steps.</p><p>Separate organic posts from paid campaigns where the available reports allow it. Compare similar content formats and record the definition behind each metric. If you calculate an engagement rate, state which interactions you counted and what you divided them by; rates using followers and rates using reach are not interchangeable.</p><p>A small dashboard should help you decide what to do. Start with a few measures you can explain rather than collecting every number available.</p></section>
<section><h2>2. Separate reach from repeated exposure</h2><p>Reach generally describes the distinct accounts or people exposed to content within the platform's definition. Impressions count displays and may include repeated exposure. Check the reporting tool's definitions before comparing channels.</p><p>Neither measure proves that someone understood the message, lives in your service area or intends to buy. Review the content and the relevant audience information available to you. A broadly shared post can attract attention without bringing the customers your business can serve.</p></section>
<section><h2>3. Follow the path to a qualified inquiry</h2><p>Connect the post to a relevant page and a clear next step. Consistent campaign tags can help distinguish traffic sources, but they do not capture every interaction or prove that a campaign caused a sale.</p><p>Keep website visits, contact-button clicks, delivered inquiries and confirmed bookings separate. A click on a form or calendar is not a completed lead. Check whether an inquiry fits your services and service area before treating it as a useful business outcome.</p><p>Where spending and outcome data are available, compare cost per qualified inquiry using the same reporting period and definition. Do not call that return on investment unless you also have the revenue and cost information needed to calculate it.</p></section>
<section><h2>4. Review customer feedback</h2><p>Read comments, mentions and messages for recurring questions, confusion and praise. Group themes such as service availability, pricing questions or difficulty contacting the business. Those themes can reveal content gaps that a reaction count misses.</p><p>Automated sentiment labels can help organize a large volume of feedback, but context matters. Review important examples yourself. Assign someone to respond and define when a service issue needs to move into a private conversation. Keep personal customer information out of shared reporting.</p></section>
<section><h2>5. Choose the next change</h2><p>Return to the original goal. Keep useful content, improve unclear explanations and stop repeating posts that do not serve the audience. Change a manageable number of things at once so the next review is easier to interpret.</p><p>For example, if project posts attract relevant questions but few people reach the service page, review the link and next-step language. If inquiries arrive but concern work you do not offer, clarify the service scope. These are hypotheses to test, not conclusions from engagement alone.</p></section>
<section><h2>A monthly review worksheet</h2><div class="worksheet"><ol><li><strong>Goal:</strong> What customer action or question are we focusing on?</li><li><strong>Period:</strong> Which dates and comparison period are included?</li><li><strong>Activity:</strong> Which channels, posts and paid campaigns ran?</li><li><strong>Exposure:</strong> What were reach and impressions, using the platform's definitions?</li><li><strong>Response:</strong> Which interactions and customer questions were useful?</li><li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> How many delivered inquiries or confirmed bookings fit our business?</li><li><strong>Costs and limits:</strong> What did we spend, and what could our tracking miss?</li><li><strong>Next action:</strong> What will change, who owns it and when will we review it?</li></ol></div><p>Keep definitions consistent from one review to the next. Note missing data instead of treating it as zero.</p></section>
<section><h2>Connect social media with the rest of your website</h2><p>Social content should lead to information that helps customers decide. Use our <a href="/resources/blog/defining-your-websites-purpose-and-target-audience/">website purpose and audience worksheet</a> to plan that destination. Learn about <a href="/services/social-media/">Holy Webs social media services</a> or <a href="/contact/">contact our team</a> to discuss your priorities.</p></section>
</article></div>
